Citation>Nature. 2007 Dec 6;450(7171):879-82</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18004300</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bioinformatics. 2010 Oct 1;26(19):2460-1</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20709691</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>J Environ Qual. 2013 Sep;42(5):1441-8</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24216421</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Anal Biochem. 1976 May 7;72:248-54</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">942051</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Waste Manag. 2011 May;31(5):864-70</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21324662</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Syst Appl Microbiol. 2014 May;37(3):165-9</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24685906</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bioinformatics. 2011 Nov 1;27(21):2957-63</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21903629</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Environ Microbiol. 2009 Dec;75(23):7537-41</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19801464</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>ISME J. 2012 Oct;6(10):1937-48</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22592821</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Environ Microbiol. 2012 Jul;78(13):4715-23</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22522690</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>ISME J. 2014 Sep;8(9):1945-8</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24785289</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Environ Microbiol. 1999 Sep;65(9):4064-70</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10473417</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Environ Microbiol. 2015 Sep;17(9):3233-50</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25581131</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Environ Microbiol. 1992 Jul;58(7):2231-6</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">1637160</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">28330207</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2020</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>01</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">2190-572X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>6</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2016</Year><Month>Dec</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>3 Biotech</Title><ISOAbbreviation>3 Biotech</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A pmoA-based study reveals dominance of yet uncultured Type I methanotrophs in rhizospheres of an organically fertilized rice field in India.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>135</StartPage><MedlinePgn>135</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">135</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s13205-016-0453-3</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Rice fields are one of the important sources of anthropogenic methane. Methanotrophs can oxidize up to 30&#xa0;% of the produced methane and thus have a pivotal environmental role in methane mitigation. India occupies the largest region under rice cultivation; however, most of the studies done on methanotrophic communities have focused on the Northern region. We studied methanotrophic community of a flooded, organically fertilized rice field using pmoA clone library approach. Organic rice fields impose a more serious threat as they produce more methane. pmoA gene is the main functional gene which is primarily used for taxonomical analysis of methanotrophs. Our results showed that the pmoA clone libraries from two growth stages of rice were dominated by pmoA sequences which were very distant from cultivated Type Ia methanotrophic genera (80-82&#xa0;% nucleotide similarity) indicative of the presence of a putatively novel genus. We designated this group of clones as 'rice field clones' as this also includes many pmoA sequences originating from other rice fields. Thus, our current knowledge of methanotroph diversity from Indian rice fields has been expanded revealing that a substantial portion of methanotrophic diversity is unexplored.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pandit</LastName><ForeName>Pranitha S</ForeName><Initials>PS</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MACS Agharkar Research Institute, G.G. Agarkar Road, Pune, Maharashtra, 411004, India.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ranade</LastName><ForeName>Dilip R</ForeName><Initials>DR</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MACS Agharkar Research Institute, G.G. Agarkar Road, Pune, Maharashtra, 411004, India.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Microbial Culture Collection, NCCS, Sai-Trinity Building, Pashan, Pune, Maharashtra, 411021, India.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dhakephalkar</LastName><ForeName>Prashant K</ForeName><Initials>PK</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MACS Agharkar Research Institute, G.G. Agarkar Road, Pune, Maharashtra, 411004, India.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Rahalkar</LastName><ForeName>Monali C</ForeName><Initials>MC</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MACS Agharkar Research Institute, G.G. Agarkar Road, Pune, Maharashtra, 411004, India. monalirahalkar@aripune.org.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D002363">Case Reports</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2016</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>16</Day></ArticleDate></